<p>{{ site.name }} publishes reported stories about artificial intelligence. The desk is operated by {{ site.legalName }}. A story can be added by committing one Markdown file. The sourcing rules on this page still apply to that file.</p>
<h2>What we publish</h2>
<p>Stories belong in one section: Models &amp; Research, Industry &amp; Business, Policy &amp; Regulation, Tools &amp; Products, or Funding. Analysis has to be identifiable in the copy. A product announcement is not rewritten as an independent test, and a company’s safety claim is not rewritten as a finding of fact.</p>
<h2>Sourcing</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Every factual claim a reasonable reader would rely on traces to a named, linkable primary source or a reputable secondary source, and that source is linked in the story.</li>
  <li>Quotes are short, attributed, and taken from a named published source. The desk does not invent quotations, numbers, dates, or events, and it does not present a vendor’s customer story as its own interview.</li>
  <li>The wording is the desk’s. A source is not copied and is not closely paraphrased. If a passage cannot be said in original words without drifting from the source, it is left out.</li>
  <li>If a fact cannot be checked, it is not published.</li>
</ul>
<p>The source list in the story’s front matter is rendered at the bottom of the page and in the NewsArticle citation metadata. Each story’s byline shows a last-updated date.</p>
<h2>People, companies, and labels</h2>
<p>The desk does not state or imply crime, misconduct, or other wrongdoing by a named person or company unless an authoritative source says it. When that happens, the story attributes the statement and does not add speculation about a person’s motives or private conduct. Opinion, analysis, and speculation are labeled as this desk’s reading.</p>
<h2>Images</h2>
<p>All images on this site are AI-generated and illustrative only. They are not documentary photographs. They do not depict a real, identifiable person or a news event that did not happen. A third-party or press image is used only when it is small, credited, and plainly fair use.</p>
<h2>Methodology, announcements, and update dates</h2>
<p>Each story is written from the primary documents it links, such as a company post, a research paper, a regulator’s notice, or a survey report, and the sources list at the end of the story names them. This desk does not independently test products or re-run benchmarks. When a figure or claim comes from the company that made it, the story says so and attributes it to that company. It is not presented as independently verified unless the story names an independent source that checked it. Every story shows the date it was last updated, and a correction is noted on the story when one is made. Nothing on the site promises a result or replaces professional advice.</p>
<h2>Advice</h2>
<p>Stories do not state health, legal, or financial advice as fact. A story that touches those subjects says on the page that it is not advice, and the <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a> applies.</p>
<h2>Corrections</h2>
<p>A factual error is fixed in the article and called out in a correction note at the top of the story. The note says what changed. Send corrections from the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> with the URL, the error, and the fix.</p>
<p>The byline shows the last-updated time on every story. A grammar fix, or a new sentence that does not change a fact, updates that time and does not get a correction note.</p>
<h2>Conflicts</h2>
<p>Stories are published by {{ site.legalName }}. If a story is about the publisher, or about a product the publisher sells, the copy says so in the first few paragraphs. No story on this site is an advertisement unless it is labeled as one. There are no advertisements today.</p>
<h2>Tools and automation</h2>
<p>A person or an automated contributor may draft a file. Either way, the published story has to meet this policy. Using a writing tool does not relax the ban on invented facts. The byline is the desk, {{ site.name }}, unless a story names a person as the author.</p>
<h2>Takedowns</h2>
<p>A request to unpublish needs to say what is false or unlawful and why a correction would not fix it. Copyright complaints should identify the work and the passage. The desk does not remove a story only because the subject dislikes the coverage.</p>
